{"attack": true, "case": "The target claim asserts that preferring better-corroborated theories is non-inductive because it 'makes no claim about the future probability of the theory being true.' But the claim's actual justification — that the survived theory is 'the best available target for future criticism' — is a forward-looking normative judgment. The claim offers two reasons: high empirical content and survival of severe tests. However, empirical content is a logical property of a theory's form, independent of its testing history (a theory can have maximal content and be falsified on its first test), so content alone cannot explain the preference for corroborated (survived) theories over untested theories of equal content. The only factor that does the explanatory work is 'has not yet been falsified' — past survival. Elevating a theory to 'best target for future criticism' on the basis of past survival constitutes an inductive inference: it assumes that a theory's resistance to past testing is a reason to prioritize it for future testing. This directly triggers the claim's own countercondition 1, which concedes that if the preference entails an assumption that the future resembles the past, it is 'indefensible on Popperian terms.' The claim thus fails its own stated test.", "counterexample": [{"description": "Two theories T1 and T2 with identical empirical content. T1 has survived severe tests; T2 is untested. The claim says we should prefer T1 as 'best available target for future criticism.' Since both have identical content, the preference is based solely on T1's survival record. This means past survival is being treated as relevant to future test-worthiness — an inductive assumption. The claim cannot escape by appealing to content (content is equal). The only remaining escape is to deny that we should prefer T1 over T2, but then 'better-corroborated' does no justificatory work and the thesis becomes vacuous."}], "premise": "The claim's justification for preferring better-corroborated theories combines 'higher empirical content' with 'has not yet been falsified,' but empirical content is a logical property of a theory independent of its survival record, so it cannot explain why survival of tests adds value. The only distinguishing factor between a survived high-content theory and an untested high-content theory is the survival itself, and treating past survival as a reason for preferring future testing is the inductive assumption the claim's own countercondition 1 identifies as fatal.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "eef13dcf71bdf67d09746bc6d6afa4e27421662e70e52728f6b803ce1d693478", "quote": "we prefer the theory that has survived severe tests because it has higher empirical content and has not yet been falsified, making it the best available target for future criticism"}, {"block": "eef13dcf71bdf67d09746bc6d6afa4e27421662e70e52728f6b803ce1d693478", "quote": "If one can demonstrate that acting on the best-corroborated theory logically entails an assumption that the future will resemble the past (induction), then the preference is indefensible on Popperian terms."}], "successor_question": "If empirical content is a logical property independent of testing history, what non-inductive reason can Popper give for preferring a high-content theory that has survived severe tests over an equally high-content theory that has not yet been tested?"}